Considerations
•	Context Mapping: A shared @context must define JEP30 terms (e.g., jep30:Footprint) to ensure consistency.
•	Data Validation: Tools like JSON-LD processors and SHACL (Shapes Constraint Language) validate structural and semantic compliance with JEP30.
•	Modularity: JEP30’s GeneratedECAD-ModelsSection can be represented as separate JSON-LD graphs for footprints, symbols, and terminal mappings.
 
4. Benefits of JSON-LD
•	Backward Compatibility: Existing JSON tools work seamlessly.
•	Linked Data Integration: JEP30 part models become part of the Semantic Web, enabling federated queries.
•	Evolvability: New JEP30 schema versions can be adopted by updating the @context without breaking existing clients.
 
5. Tools & Standards
•	JSON-LD Playground: Test transformations between JSON and JSON-LD.
•	JEP30-M101 Schema: Defines XML structure for validation.
•	RDF Converters: Tools like rdf2jsonld bridge XML-based JEP30 and JSON-LD.
By leveraging JSON-LD’s semantic capabilities, translation between JSON datasheets and JEP30 achieves full fidelity while maintaining compatibility with existing infrastructure.
